<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> winter 2026-27 is a strong El Niño, not a La Niña. NOAA gives a better than 79% chance of a strong to very strong El Niño through January to March 2027. Its November winter outlook favors a warmer north and a wetter south from California to Florida, and a drier Northern Rockies. In past strong El Niños, California, Utah, Colorado and the Southwest got more snow, and the Pacific Northwest to the Great Lakes got less.</p></div>

<h2>Is this winter El Niño or La Niña?</h2>
<p>El Niño. NOAA's Climate Prediction Center has an El Niño Advisory in effect. Its November 12, 2026 update said El Niño "continues to strengthen" and gave a better than 79% chance of a strong to very strong event through January to March 2027. The Niño-3.4 ocean index reached +2.5°C. CPC put the odds of a historic event, bigger than any since 1950 (+2.5°C or more), at about 60% for October to December and November to January, down from 83% in October but still better than even.</p>
<p>CPC expects El Niño to peak this winter and weaken in early 2027, with neutral conditions more likely by spring. It also cautions that with an event this big, El Niño-like impacts are more likely "though not guaranteed." Its next updates come out December 10 (ENSO) and December 17 (seasonal outlook); we'll update this page after each.</p>

<h2>What NOAA's winter outlook says (December to February)</h2>
<p>From NOAA's November 19, 2026 outlook. CPC says it has more confidence in the precipitation outlook than the temperature outlook this winter, because a strong El Niño has a reliable wet pattern.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Warmer than average</strong> is favored across the northern two-thirds of the US. The odds are highest (over 60%) from northwestern Oregon through Washington, and over 50% in the Northeast and Great Lakes.</li>
  <li><strong>Wetter than average</strong> is favored from southwestern Oregon and California through the Southwest, the southern Plains, the Southeast, the Mid-Atlantic and much of the Northeast. NOAA raised the wet odds for California and the Desert Southwest in November; southern California tops 60%.</li>
  <li><strong>Drier than average</strong> is favored in the Northern Rockies (over 50%), parts of the Great Lakes and the northern Plains.</li>
  <li><strong>Utah and Colorado:</strong> not singled out. They sit between the wetter Southwest and the drier Northern Rockies.</li>
  <li>NOAA's outlook does not forecast snowfall totals. It forecasts the odds of above or below normal temperature and precipitation.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What a strong El Niño has meant for ski regions</h2>
<p>NOAA's Climate.gov looked at snowfall during El Niño winters from 1950 to 2009. The most reliable signal is <strong>below-average snow from the Pacific Northwest to the Great Lakes</strong>. In <strong>strong</strong> El Niños, California, Utah, Colorado, Arizona and New Mexico were among the states with the biggest above-average snow, though that rests on only about 10 strong events.</p>
